'''''Fumaria agraria''''' is an herbaceous [[annual plant]] in the poppy family [[Papaveraceae]]. It is native to [[North Africa]] and the [[Southern Europe|western Mediterranean Europe]], and has been introduced to [[Chile]], [[Peru]], [[Ecuador]], and [[Argentina|northern Argentina]], where it is now an invasive species.<ref name=distribution>{{cite web|url=https://powo.science.kew.org/taxon/urn:lsid:ipni.org:names:672858-1#distribution-map|title=agraria distribution map |publisher=Royal Botanic Gardens Kew |accessdate=22 February 2022}}</ref>

==Description==
The leaves are [[leaf shape|pinnatisect]]. The [[Sepal|sepals]] measure 3.5–5.5 mm long and 1–2 mm wide, the petals  are 12–16 mm long and white to slightly pinkish, with only the inner petals showing dark purple tips. The lower petal has a spreading edge. The fruit of the plant is 2.5–3.5 mm in both length and width. <ref>{{Cite web |last=Mifsud |first=Stephen |date=2002-08-23 |title=Fumaria agraria (Field Fumitory) : MaltaWildPlants.com - the online Flora of the Maltese Islands. |url=https://www.maltawildplants.com/FUMR/Fumaria_agraria.php#NOM |access-date=2026-04-05 |website=www.maltawildplants.com |language=en-us}}</ref>

